1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,968
The tide API flakes. Not always — just
enough that berth planners see timeouts on
calm mornings. You do not want every
caller to invent its own retry loop.

2
00:00:11,087 --> 00:00:24,695
You want one place that says: around tide
fetches, retry a couple of times, then
surface the failure. That “around” is
advice. A proxy gives you a place to
stand; advice is what you do while
standing there.

3
00:00:24,815 --> 00:00:37,439
Spring’s annotation model maps cleanly
onto spoken intent: before, after
returning, after throwing, after finally,
and around. Look at this java for a
moment. Walk each kind.

4
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:49,008
@Before runs before the target; it can
throw and stop the call — here a red
station board freezes forecasts.
@AfterReturning runs only on success and
can bind the returned TideWindow.

5
00:00:49,128 --> 00:00:59,064
@AfterThrowing runs only when the target
throws, binding the exception type you
care about. @After is the finally analog —
good for clearing thread state.

6
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:10,343
@Around is the power tool: receive a
ProceedingJoinPoint, choose whether to
call proceed, call it multiple times for
retry, replace arguments, replace the
return value, or wrap exceptions.

7
00:01:10,463 --> 00:01:26,256
The flaky tide retry lives naturally here.
Transaction management is around-style in
spirit: begin before proceed, commit or
roll back after. Security checks are often
before or around. Timing is naturally
around.

8
00:01:26,376 --> 00:01:36,384
Prefer the weakest advice that expresses
intent. If you only need to log a return
value, @AfterReturning is clearer than an
@Around that always proceeds.

9
00:01:36,504 --> 00:01:44,712
Order among advice kinds on the same join
point follows Spring’s rules; when
multiple aspects apply you need explicit
aspect ordering later.

10
00:01:44,832 --> 00:01:53,952
Inside one @Around, your code’s structure
is the order: before proceed is before;
after is after. Misuse patterns are
familiar.

11
00:01:54,072 --> 00:02:08,183
Calling proceed twice by accident doubles
side effects — fine for deliberate retry,
disastrous for a charge. Forgetting
proceed silently skips business logic.
Heavy domain work in @Before muddies the
aspect.

12
00:02:08,304 --> 00:02:15,432
Swallowing exceptions inside @Around
without rethrow can bypass @AfterThrowing
and transactional rollback you thought you
had.

13
00:02:15,551 --> 00:02:27,407
Advice answers “what happens at the join
point.” It does not answer “which join
points.” Without a precise selector, the
same advice might attach to every public
method — or to nothing.

14
00:02:27,527 --> 00:02:30,840
Aiming the advice is the job of pointcuts.
